00:00:48.556 --> 00:00:53.500
Watching this Peranema feels a bit like watching
a cat waffling back and forth between whether

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.707
or not it wants to take a nap.

00:00:55.707 --> 00:01:01.899
Sometimes the Peranema stretches, its body
undulating into an elongated, indescribable

00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:05.842
geometry as its flagella twitch like whiskers.

00:01:05.842 --> 00:01:12.170
And then, sometimes, it curls up into a cozy
circle, tucking one end into itself the way

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:17.724
any feline friend you might know curls up
around the perfect beam of sunshine.

00:01:17.724 --> 00:01:23.171
When James, our master of microscopes, began
his journey with his first microscope, he

00:01:23.171 --> 00:01:27.000
found Peranema in their elongated form.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.420
But for some reason, whenever he went to record
them under higher magnification, the Peranema

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:36.780
reverted to their curled-up form, and James
did not know why.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:41.790
He guessed—like many microscopists before
him—that this behavior had something to

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:44.850
do with the intensity of the light.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:50.570
After all, switching to a higher magnification
on the microscope requires shining more light

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:51.930
on your sample.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:57.473
And the more light that James shone on the
Peranema, the more he saw it curl up.

00:01:57.473 --> 00:02:02.920
When we looked more into the bits and pieces
inside of Peranema, we were surprised to find

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:07.714
that it actually had something in common with
us, and with how we see the world.

00:02:07.714 --> 00:02:12.520
It’s always thrilling to find something
in common with microbes, even if it shouldn’t

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.122
be surprising.

00:02:14.122 --> 00:02:19.170
If you go back far enough in the evolutionary
history of human beings, you will hit upon

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:24.494
the ancestors we share with Peranema or other
present-day microbes.

00:02:24.494 --> 00:02:30.520
And sometimes we have similar traits not because
we inherited them from the same ancestor,

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:36.820
but because nature itself is repetitive and
finds ways to make motifs out of chemicals.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:40.809
And sometimes, evolution is a funny combination
of both of those things.

00:02:41.521 --> 00:02:47.349
Take, for example, the family of organisms
that Peranema belongs to, the euglenoids,

00:02:47.349 --> 00:02:52.290
a perennial favorite on Journey to the Microcosmos
because of the fascinating shapes they take

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:58.830
on, thanks to a series of strips made out
of protein that run the length of the organism.

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:03.709
If you are new to the world of euglenoids,
you may notice something starkly different

00:03:03.709 --> 00:03:05.407
in some of these species.

00:03:05.407 --> 00:03:11.080
Some of them are a very vivid green, which
makes them stand out like bright patches of

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:15.000
grass against an otherwise flat background.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.739
And this sets them in distinct opposition
to colorless euglenoids like the Peranema,

00:03:19.739 --> 00:03:24.989
which scrunch and wiggle and dance in the
same unusual way, but they are definitely

00:03:24.989 --> 00:03:26.442
not green.

00:03:26.442 --> 00:03:31.440
Now other than this very definitive color
difference, colorless euglenoids share a number

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:34.970
of traits in common with their green counterparts.

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:40.920
They use their flagella to glide and swim,
most use the constant shifting of their bodies

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:46.008
as a way to move around the microcosmos, a
motion called metaboly.

00:03:46.008 --> 00:03:49.846
So why are some green and others are not?

00:03:49.846 --> 00:03:55.768
Well the answer goes back to some ancient
euglenoid that developed its own photosynthetic

00:03:55.768 --> 00:04:01.534
organelle, not by making it from scratch,
but by absorbing it from something else.

00:04:01.534 --> 00:04:06.990
Most likely, it consumed some kind of green
algae millions of years ago, and then instead

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:13.519
of digesting its meal, absorbed the algae
and its chloroplasts into its own body until

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:16.030
they became one organism.

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:20.620
The process by which these green euglenoids
got their photosynthetic plastids is called

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:28.009
secondary endosymbiosis, an absorption of
an algae that had absorbed a cyanobacteria

00:04:28.009 --> 00:04:34.808
like a nesting doll of photosynthesis, or
a poem that references itself as it unfolds.

00:04:34.808 --> 00:04:40.520
And as far as motifs of the microcosmos go,
the recurrence of endosymbiosis is one of

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:44.320
the most dominant and consequential.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:48.358
We have seen it over and over again as we
explore the evolution of

00:04:48.358 --> 00:04:51.180
mitochondria and chloroplasts.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:56.620
And it sets up a particularly important divide
among eukaryotes: those who can make their

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:59.750
own food, and those who cannot.

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:04.840
The green euglenoids with their photosynthetic
plastids are able to make their own food,

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:08.368
converting sunlight into the molecules that
sustain them and form the

00:05:08.368 --> 00:05:11.721
basis for entire ecosystems.

00:05:11.721 --> 00:05:14.199
But the Peranema, they’re like us.

00:05:14.199 --> 00:05:18.382
They cannot make their own food, so they have
to hunt it down.

00:05:18.382 --> 00:05:19.699
And they’re very good at it.

00:05:19.699 --> 00:05:24.630
If an organism is small enough, the Peranema
will simply swallow it whole.

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:29.370
And if their prey is too large for that, the
Peranema will use a part of its mouth called

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:34.450
the rod-organ to cut up its meal into a more
digestible format.

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:40.699
And this need to gather or hunt our food is
not the only thing we have in common with Peranema.

00:05:40.699 --> 00:05:45.250
As we said in the beginning, that unusual
curling behavior we saw happening has its

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:50.564
roots in something else we share with these
unusual microbes: rhodopsin.

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:56.720
For humans—and in fact for many animals—rhodopsin
is a protein that plays an important role

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:02.600
in our ability to see things, converting the
light that hits it into an electrical signal

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:05.500
that our brains can process into an image.

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:11.060
And rhodopsin proteins with similar sequences
have been found in all kinds of organisms,

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:14.600
including green algae and fungi.

00:06:15.103 --> 00:06:18.840
And—it turns out—they are also found in
Peranema.

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:24.000
But the Peranema doesn’t see with its rhodopsin
receptors—at least not the way that we do.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:30.770
Instead, when light hits the rhodopsin receptors,
it sets off an influx of calcium ions, which

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:34.900
in turns drives the organism to curl up into
a ball.

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:40.430
This behavior is similar to the way that bacteria
and archaea use rhodopsins to control their

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:44.510
tendency to reverse directions when they come
across light.

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:50.130
And the fact that this protein appears again
and again in different organisms makes it

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:56.600
a fascinating way to learn more about how
evolution has shaped and reshaped similar

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.538
components into different tools.

00:06:59.538 --> 00:07:01.954
Because sure, something might be common.

00:07:01.954 --> 00:07:04.324
But that doesn’t mean it can’t be fascinating.

00:07:04.324 --> 00:07:07.577
Indeed, very often, it means the opposite.

